WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:05.920
The Marvel Cinematic Universe only scratches
the surface when it comes to Mjolnir and Stormbreaker.

00:00:06.170 --> 00:00:11.220
From their legendary origins to their modern-day
enchantments, here’s the truth behind Thor’s

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:11.980
magical weapons.

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:16.700
You might already know this, but the god of
thunder goes back way further than their Marvel

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:20.640
Comics debut in 1962, and so does his thunderous
hammer.

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:26.060
Mjolnir got its origin story
in the 13th-century Prose Edda, and like most

00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:29.060
Norse mythology, it involves Loki causing
trouble.

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:34.440
In this case, he made a wager with a pair
of dwarves named Brokkr and Sindri you may

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:39.750
know them from the 2018 game God of War betting
that they couldn't craft three magical items

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:43.200
that were the equal of Asgard's three greatest
treasures.

00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:47.720
To rise to the challenge, the blacksmithing
dwarves set about working their forge and,

00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:51.600
despite Loki's interference, they crafted
a trio of treasures of their own.

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:56.320
The most well-known, of course, was Mjolnir,
which turned out to be a pretty good hammer.

00:00:57.080 --> 00:01:02.000
As powerful as it was, Mjolnir wasn't Thor's
only piece of equipment in the myths.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:06.990
To heft the incredibly heavy hammer, a problem
that he had to face long before Marvel Comics

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:10.220
came along, he had to use another pair of
magic items.

00:01:10.549 --> 00:01:16.180
The first was the Jarngreipr, a pair of gauntlets
known as the "Iron Grippers," and the second

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:21.320
was the Megingjord, a belt that doubled his
already considerable strength.

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:26.940
"We just gotta load Tony's old Hulkbuster
armor, the prototype for Cap's new shield

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:28.520
and the Megin..

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:29.790
The megin…

00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:31.580
Thor's magic belt."

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:36.200
Over the years, Marvel has added or adapted
most of the mythological stories behind the

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:40.619
Norse gods, so it's not surprising that both
of those items exist in the comics.

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:46.280
However, the Megingjord shows up rarely on
the page, and to explain why Thor doesn't

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:52.069
just wear it all the time to double his strength,
Marvel's version, according to Thor #363,

00:01:52.069 --> 00:01:54.560
takes a pretty serious toll on the god of
thunder.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:02:00.320
Marvel's version of Mjolnir first appeared
in Journey into Mystery #83, loaded up with

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:04.340
a few interesting spells to make for more
exciting superhero comic stories.

00:02:04.869 --> 00:02:09.820
The first, of course, was the familiar enchantment
by Odin that meant only the "worthy" could

00:02:09.820 --> 00:02:10.740
lift it.

00:02:10.820 --> 00:02:15.940
"Whosoever holds this hammer, if he be worthy,
shall possess the power of Thor."

00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:20.840
For the other, though, it seems like the Marvel
masterminds may have been taking some inspiration

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:24.980
from a lightning-powered hero who was a little
more recent than the Prose Edda.

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:30.510
In the Golden Age of Comics, young Billy Batson
discovered a wizard in a cave and said the

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:36.870
magic word "SHAZAM!" to transform with a thunderous
blast into Captain Marvel, the World's Mightiest

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:37.360
Mortal.

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:41.720
He even shared his power with his sidekick,
Captain Marvel Jr., whose secret identity

00:02:41.740 --> 00:02:46.180
was Freddy Freeman, a young man whose injured
leg led him to walk with a crutch.

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:50.120
Captain Marvel stopped being published in
the '50s, though he would eventually come

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:54.180
back as DC's Shazam, renamed for various legal
reasons.

00:02:54.460 --> 00:02:58.280
Fans might've found an interesting alternative
in 1962, though.

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:03.000
Marvel's version of Thor was secretly Don
Blake, a doctor whose injured leg led him

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:04.190
to walk with a cane.

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:09.120
He discovered a stout stick in a cave, and
when he smacked it against the ground,it turned

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:15.460
into Mjolnir and transformed him with a thunderous
blast into Thor, the World's Mightiest Immortal.

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:18.760
Blake was then blessed with the strength and
power of Thor.

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:24.140
Whether the Marvel bullpen was actually trying
to recapture the magic of Captain Marvel Adventures

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:28.100
by giving it some of that hot Viking flavor
is certainly up for debate.

00:03:28.660 --> 00:03:33.100
Marvel's most enduring addition to the Mjolnir
mythos was the enchantment that granted the

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:36.080
power of Thor to those who were worthy of
it.

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:38.020
Anyone who was worthy of it.

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:42.520
Over the years, that's a list that has included
a surprising number of names, but the more

00:03:42.530 --> 00:03:47.079
notable examples include Steve Rogers, who
first hefted the hammer when he was operating

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:52.340
as "The Captain," and later lifted it in his
more familiar identity of Captain America.

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:54.560
"...assemble."

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:59.700
Squirrel Girl also got her hands on the hammer
and used it to stop a misguided duplicate

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:03.460
of herself who couldn't lift the hammer from
beating up the Marvel Universe.

00:04:03.670 --> 00:04:09.380
The most eye-catching for crossover fans,
however, came at the climax of JLA/Avengers,

00:04:09.389 --> 00:04:14.560
when Superman charged into battle wielding
both Captain America's shield and Thor's hammer

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:15.500
to save the day.

00:04:16.060 --> 00:04:20.540
Once the battle was over, however, Superman
found himself unable to lift it, leading Thor

00:04:20.540 --> 00:04:25.860
to explain that Odin occasionally sees fit
to suspend the enchantment for special occasions.

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:33.800
In 1983, Walt Simonson took over as the writer
and artist of Thor with #337, and he certainly

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:35.400
brought in plenty of new ideas.

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:36.700
The first big shift?

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:42.400
Ditching the Thunder God's human identity
and making the new Thor a space horse.

00:04:42.700 --> 00:04:45.680
Okay, it's slightly more complicated than
that.

00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:50.340
While investigating something in space, Thor
encounters an alien warrior named Beta Ray

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:50.960
Bill.

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:56.340
In the fight, Thor loses his hammer, and thanks
to yet another enchantment that required him

00:04:56.340 --> 00:05:01.540
to keep in contact with it to maintain his
godly form, it turns back into Blake's walking

00:05:01.540 --> 00:05:02.040
stick.

00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:07.120
A very frustrated Bill grabs the stick and
smacks it against the wall, and readers suddenly

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:12.950
learned that this strange new alien who definitely
seemed like a bad guy is every bit as worthy

00:05:12.950 --> 00:05:14.960
as the guy with his name on the cover.

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:21.000
Bill transformed into a new, sci-fi Thor,
and was promptly whisked off to Asgard because

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:25.020
Odin's summoning spell was looking for "Thor,"
not his son.

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:29.860
Over the next few issues, Bill has to battle
to the death against Thor to determine who

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:34.460
would be the true god of thunder — which,
if we're being honest here, is some pretty

00:05:34.460 --> 00:05:36.420
rough parenting from Odin the All-Father.

00:05:37.130 --> 00:05:41.200
In the end, though, Bill ended up saving Thor's
life and proving himself to be a good and

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:44.520
noble soul who just happened to look like
a giant skeletal horse.

00:05:45.250 --> 00:05:50.940
Bill swore his loyalty to Thor as his "oath-brother,"
and to reward his nobility, Odin had Eitri

00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:55.400
the dwarf craft a new hammer to be Mjolnir's
equal: Stormbreaker.

00:05:55.840 --> 00:06:01.280
It was every bit as strong, had the same enchantment
about worthiness, and the only big difference

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:02.900
came in the aesthetics.

00:06:02.900 --> 00:06:08.440
Stormbreaker, while made of the same Uru metal
as Mjolnir, appeared golden and had one flat

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:11.600
hammer-head and an ax-like blade on the other
side.

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:17.120
Beta Ray Bill isn't the only strange transformation
that Thor underwent during Walt Simonson's

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:18.100
five-year run.

00:06:18.380 --> 00:06:23.300
At one point in the comics, Thor found himself
turned into a frog thanks to — let's say

00:06:23.300 --> 00:06:25.660
it all together now — Loki.

00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:30.720
While he was a frog, he got caught in a Secret
of NIMH style war between New York City's

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:35.270
frogs and the ravenous sewer rat population
that was being controlled by a bad guy with

00:06:35.270 --> 00:06:37.540
low aspirations called the Piper.

00:06:37.780 --> 00:06:41.800
The whole issue was built around a struggle
for Thor to recover his hammer, and when he

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:47.290
did, he struck it against the ground and was
thunderously transformed into … a six-foot

00:06:47.290 --> 00:06:49.480
six-inch frog dressed like Thor.

00:06:49.860 --> 00:06:51.820
That's not the end of the frog's story, though.

00:06:52.100 --> 00:06:56.900
While he was briefly amphibious, Thor spent
some time hanging out with a frog named Puddlegulp,

00:06:56.910 --> 00:07:01.610
who it turned out was also a human transformed
into a frog, because that's the kind of thing

00:07:01.610 --> 00:07:04.380
that happens when you're living in a universe
full of wizards.

00:07:05.110 --> 00:07:09.740
At one point, however, Puddlegulp found a
shard of Mjolnir that was chipped off by one

00:07:09.740 --> 00:07:12.020
of Thor's chariot-pulling goats.

00:07:12.340 --> 00:07:17.980
Puddlegulp lifted the shard, struck it against
the ground, and became Throg, the frog of

00:07:17.980 --> 00:07:18.540
thunder.

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:25.020
Unlike Thor's time as the thunder frog, Throg's
was still frog-sized, with a tiny Mjolnir

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:25.600
to match.

00:07:25.860 --> 00:07:30.180
This hammer had all the abilities of the regular
version, and was thus given the extremely

00:07:30.180 --> 00:07:32.660
on-the-nose name of "Frogjolnir."

00:07:33.160 --> 00:07:38.240
In 2014, Thor came to the conclusion that
he was no longer worthy to wield Mjolnir,

00:07:38.240 --> 00:07:41.960
and that lack of confidence became a self-fulfilling
prophecy.

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:46.480
While Thor was being glum about this turn
of events — and getting his left arm chopped

00:07:46.480 --> 00:07:51.800
off by Malekith the Dark Elf in a pretty disastrous
attempt at hammerless crime-fighting — Jane

00:07:51.800 --> 00:07:57.800
Foster, believing that there must always be
a thunder god to stand against evil, hefted

00:07:58.200 --> 00:07:59.720
the hammer herself and became the Mighty Thor.

00:08:00.760 --> 00:08:05.180
For the next three years, Jane was Thor, but
her tenure as the goddess of thunder came

00:08:05.440 --> 00:08:08.860
to crashing end when Mjolnir itself was destroyed.

00:08:15.940 --> 00:08:20.000
It happened during a battle with the Mangog,
a creature that had come into being in a past

00:08:20.010 --> 00:08:25.070
era when Odin destroyed an entire planet,
and the pure hatred of its population coalesced

00:08:25.070 --> 00:08:27.060
into a nearly unstoppable monster.

00:08:27.380 --> 00:08:29.620
The key word there, however, is "nearly."

00:08:30.020 --> 00:08:34.360
Jane did end up stopping the Mangog by chaining
him to Mjolnir and hurling it into the heart

00:08:34.360 --> 00:08:34.980
of the sun.

00:08:35.360 --> 00:08:39.180
As you may know from science, the core of
the sun is roughly 10,000 degrees Fahrenheit

00:08:39.180 --> 00:08:41.840
and contains a constant nuclear reaction.

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:46.770
That turns out to be about the level of force
that you need in order to destroy a presumably

00:08:46.770 --> 00:08:48.540
"unbreakable" magic hammer.

00:08:49.140 --> 00:08:55.440
Mjolnir was vaporized, Jane retired from Thor-ing,
and the Odinson once again took up his heroic

00:08:55.440 --> 00:08:59.980
exploits, using a series of substitute hammers
crafted by a dwarf named Screwbeard.

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:04.560
Sadly, due to a shortage of the mystical Uru
metal from which the original Mjolnir had

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:08.860
been crafted, none of the replacements were
as good as the genuine article.

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:13.140
Mjolnir may have been destroyed forever, but
this is comics.

00:09:13.320 --> 00:09:17.300
It's only a matter of time before everyone
comes back from the dead, including hammers.

00:09:17.860 --> 00:09:24.140
In this case, it happened during 2019's War
of the Realms, a crossover event that, shockingly,

00:09:24.140 --> 00:09:25.760
was not Loki's fault.

00:09:26.020 --> 00:09:31.400
Instead, it's Malekith again, leading an invasion
of Midgard, or as we call it, Earth, from

00:09:31.400 --> 00:09:33.480
the united forces of the other realms.

00:09:33.760 --> 00:09:38.680
At the climax of the story, Thor conjures
up the God Tempest, a storm so powerful that

00:09:38.680 --> 00:09:43.660
it affects the sun itself, reforging Mjolnir
and sending it crashing back to Earth.

00:09:44.600 --> 00:09:49.240
The reborn Mjolnir was pretty recognizable
as the original, with the exception of the

00:09:49.240 --> 00:09:49.740
handle.

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:55.380
This incarnation had a handle made of braided,
natural-looking wood, presumably to bring

00:09:55.390 --> 00:09:59.800
it more into line visually with the version
of Stormbreaker that moviegoing audiences

00:09:59.800 --> 00:10:02.880
would've seen in Avengers: Infinity War and
Endgame.

00:10:03.340 --> 00:10:08.180
It also retained the worthiness enchantment,
but Thor now saw worthiness as a struggle

00:10:08.270 --> 00:10:13.580
that we all face to be better, declaring himself,
along with his new hammer, to be the god of

00:10:13.580 --> 00:10:14.580
the unworthy.

00:10:15.220 --> 00:10:19.760
While they're easily the most notable ones,
Mjolnir and Stormbreaker are far from the

00:10:19.760 --> 00:10:22.420
only Asgardian weapons to show up on the page.

00:10:22.600 --> 00:10:28.120
Most of the weapons that originated in mythological
sources are present in the world of the comics,

00:10:28.130 --> 00:10:31.960
like Odin's spear, Gungnir, and Heimdall's
magic sword, Hofund.

00:10:31.960 --> 00:10:36.950
Plus, there's Gram, the sword of the dragon-slayer
Sigmund, and Laevateinn, Loki's sword whose

00:10:36.950 --> 00:10:40.320
name has the amazing translation of "Damage
Twig."

00:10:40.580 --> 00:10:42.900
Others, however, are unique to the comics.

00:10:43.240 --> 00:10:48.150
Before he proved himself worthy of Mjolnir,
Thor used a fearsome ax named Jarnbjorn, or

00:10:48.150 --> 00:10:49.260
"Iron Bear."

00:10:50.080 --> 00:10:51.740
"Is that a Dragonfang?"

00:10:51.940 --> 00:10:52.720
"It is."

00:10:53.200 --> 00:10:54.960
"My god.

00:10:55.600 --> 00:10:56.980
It's the famed sword of the Valkyrie.”

00:10:57.620 --> 00:11:02.280
Dragonfang, which appeared on the screen in
Thor: Ragnarok, is the sword carried by Brunhilde

00:11:02.280 --> 00:11:06.860
the Valkyrie, so named because it was literally
carved from a dragon's tooth.

00:11:07.300 --> 00:11:11.460
Before he got his hands on a couple of assault
rifles, Skurge the Executioner was fond of

00:11:11.460 --> 00:11:16.300
using the Bloodaxe to behead his enemies,
and its violent name is pretty self-explanatory.

00:11:16.660 --> 00:11:18.040
There are other hammers, too.

00:11:18.100 --> 00:11:23.260
Loki once crafted a Mjolnir-like bludgeon
named Stormcaster that he gave to the X-Men's

00:11:23.260 --> 00:11:25.900
resident weather-controlling goddess, Storm.

00:11:26.300 --> 00:11:31.000
Eric Masterson briefly took on the identity
of Thor while the actual god was banished,

00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:36.160
and when the Odinson returned, Eric became
a hero named Thunderstrike, wielding a magical

00:11:36.160 --> 00:11:37.500
mace of the same name.

00:11:37.600 --> 00:11:43.640
Most recently, Jane Foster took up a mystical
Asgardian artifact named Undrjarn the All-Weapon,

00:11:43.640 --> 00:11:48.300
which can transform into any weapon that Jane
required in her new job as a Valkyrie.

00:11:48.600 --> 00:11:50.020
Oh, and the Asgardian name?

00:11:50.340 --> 00:11:55.820
Fittingly enough, for a weapon unique to the
comics page, it translates loosely to "Marvelous

00:11:55.820 --> 00:11:56.820
Weapon."

